<h2>The three functions of the invocation notice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Limitation.</strong> Receipt by the respondent commences the arbitration for the purposes of Section 43 read with the Limitation Act — the single most valuable date in the claimant's chronology.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Definition of the reference.</strong> The disputes described in the notice are the disputes "referred"; the tribunal's mandate and later challenges to jurisdiction are measured against this description.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Activation of the appointment machinery.</strong> The notice triggers the agreed procedure — party nominations, institutional rules, or the thirty-day window whose expiry opens the door to a court appointment under Section 11.</div>
</div>

<h2>What a sound notice contains</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>The agreement:</strong> identification of the contract and the arbitration clause, quoted or annexed, including seat, institutional rules and tribunal composition as agreed;</li>
  <li><strong>The disputes:</strong> a structured statement of each claim — the transaction, the breach, the amount or relief, and the period to which it relates — broad enough to cover the case, specific enough to define it;</li>
  <li><strong>The demand:</strong> an unambiguous request that the disputes be referred to arbitration — the operative words on which commencement law turns;</li>
  <li><strong>The appointment step:</strong> nomination or proposal of arbitrator(s) per the clause, with a call upon the respondent to act within thirty days of receipt;</li>
  <li><strong>Reservations:</strong> interest claims, the right to supplement particulars, and interim-measure rights under Section 9 preserved expressly.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Service: where invocations fail</h2>
<p>Commencement occurs on <em>receipt</em>, so proof of receipt is the notice's evidentiary spine. Section 3 of the Act supplies the default rule on when a written communication is deemed received — delivery to the addressee personally or at the place of business, habitual residence or mailing address, and, where none can be found after reasonable inquiry, dispatch to the last-known address by registered letter or equivalent means providing a record of the attempt. Practice should exceed the minimum: service by speed post with tracking, by email to the addresses used in the contract's dealings, and by courier, with every receipt preserved. Where the respondent is a company, service at the registered office anchors the record; where the clause designates notice addresses, they must be used. Enforcement-stage battles over commencement are almost always battles over service records assembled — or not assembled — years earlier.</p>

<h2>The timeline the notice sets running</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Day 0.</strong> Respondent receives the invocation: arbitration commences for the disputes described; limitation stops for them.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Days 1–30.</strong> The respondent acts under the agreed mechanism — concurring in a nomination, making its own, or engaging the institution. A respondent's silence is itself legally significant.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Day 31 onward.</strong> On failure of the mechanism, the claimant may apply for appointment — for international commercial arbitration and for other arbitrations as the Act allocates, through the court framework under Section 11, in Delhi ordinarily before the High Court for arbitrations seated there.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Parallel track.</strong> Interim measures under Section 9 remain available before or during the proceedings; an invocation already served strengthens the Section 9 court's comfort that arbitration is genuinely underway.</div>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Draft the invocation as the first pleading, not the last letter. It will be annexure A to the Section 11 application, exhibit one before the tribunal, and the document against which limitation and jurisdiction are tested at the Section 34 and enforcement stages. Rushed invocations that describe the disputes in a single sweeping sentence purchase speed at the cost of years of argument about what was actually referred.</p>
</div>

<h2>Common errors and their costs</h2>
<p>Recurring failures include: notices demanding payment with arbitration mentioned only as a threat — which may not amount to a request to refer, leaving commencement unachieved; notices served on a subsidiary, sister concern or individual officer rather than the contracting party; nominations made in breach of the agreed mechanism, seeding a Section 12 or Section 16 challenge to the tribunal's very constitution; invocations that omit whole heads of claim, forcing later arguments about supplementary references; and, most expensively, invocations dispatched days before limitation expires to addresses at which receipt cannot be proved. Each error is curable on the day of drafting and nearly incurable afterwards.</p>

<h2>The takeaway</h2>
<p>The Section 21 notice repays the care given to it many times over: it fixes the limitation date, frames the reference, and disciplines the appointment process. The working standard is simple — a notice that a stranger could read and know exactly what contract, what disputes, what demand and what next step — served so that receipt is beyond argument. Arbitration rewards parties who begin precisely; the invocation is where precision begins.</p>
